This is code for the Renesas R5F212L4 microcontroller (R8C/2L) on the ToorCon
13 badge.

for more information: http://greatscottgadgets.com/tc13badge

Nothing here is built or tested
-------------------------------

This image is in no build: it needs an m32c-elf toolchain that nobody working
on this repository has, and nobody here has a badge to run it on. Its sources
are kept in step with the rest of the firmware by review and by the source
scans in firmware/tests, which read them as text. So every change to this
directory is reasoned, never measured.

Where a change rests on a number, the number is worked out from this part's
clock or taken from a measurement made on the Ubertooth's own copy of the same
code, and the comment beside it says which. The radio's SPI clock
(SPI_BRG_FIFO_READ) and the wait before a FIFO read (FIFO_LEAD_POLLS) are both
of that kind: the range test they are part of now frames its FIFO transfers the
way the radio's datasheet asks for, as the Ubertooth firmware does, but neither
figure has been on a scope.

What that means for the range test's result: it can now pass, where before it
could not. The FIFO was never written and the read came back as zeros, so the
radio's checksum flag was set -- that flag only says the next two bytes in the
FIFO match the CRC16 over the bytes already read, which reading zeros out of an
empty FIFO satisfies -- and then the comparison against what had been sent
failed at the first byte. So the badge reported a failure whatever the two
boards did, however close they were. Confirming a pass on a real badge still
needs a badge.

Two things the new FIFO transfers take on trust, both on this part rather than
on the radio. cc2400_spi_byte() returns zero when the UART's flag does not
come, so a FIFO read of a part whose UART has stopped is a buffer of zeros and
says nothing about it. And spi_clock(), which changes the SPI clock for a FIFO
read, switches both halves of the UART off and on around the change, trusting
the part to raise its transmit-ready flag again afterwards.
